    "done_when": "Measure first, then cut. Since the cached-availability fix, 7 of 37 ledger picks (docs/state/reckon/runs/r-2026100[23]*.json, field picker_selection, fallback_reason \"timeout\") hit the 5 s dispatch bound and fell back to the flight default lane; median latency_ms is about 2,500 and the 90th percentile sits at the bound. The Jev call itself is about 350 ms.\n\n1. Profile one real pick on the dispatch path, under the fleet load at the time (reckon crew pick --project reckon with a representative node, plus the in-dispatch path with cached_only observations), and attribute wall time to each phase: candidate snapshot, ledger read, budget snapshot, run_time_profile, local_lane_load, template render, Jev HTTP call. Record the per-phase table, from several picks, in docs/evidence/fragments/one-typesafe-model-picker/picker-pick-within-bound.html, with the figures from tool output only.\n2. Remove the dominant cost(s) so that a pick on the dispatch path completes well inside the bound. The target is a median of 1.5 s or less and no phase that scales with ledger size on the critical path. If a phase cannot be cut, state why from the measurement and propose the bound the measured distribution supports; do not raise the bound without that evidence.\n3. A timed-out pick must stay a recorded fallback, never a refusal, and the 5 s bound must still hold when Jev is slow (a mocked slow client test).\n\nGate: the picker test files (tests/test_picker*.py), tests/test_routing_picker_key.py, tests/test_routing_picker_default.py, tests/test_picker_inputs_never_block_dispatch.py and any new test file, run with the main checkout's .venv/bin/python -m pytest -p no:cacheprovider, one log under the run directory for base and one for head, judged by delta. Do not run the full dispatch population; the coordinator runs it at merge.",
